WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
You’ve probably heard a lot about tariffs
in the last couple of years.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:04.760
But what are they, really?

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:07.140
And when countries impose them, who wins?

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.420
And who loses?

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:14.460
Tariffs are essentially a tax on anything
imported.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:18.560
Businesses that buy foreign goods pay this
tax to their national governments.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:24.160
For example, if the United States imposes
a tariff on goods from China, American companies

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:28.180
importing those goods must pay U.S. customs
to bring them in.

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:29.920
After that, a few things could happen.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:36.140
The Chinese company might cut prices so potential
customers stay happy and keep buying their products.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:39.340
But let’s say the Chinese company doesn’t
cut its prices.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:45.580
In that case, the importing company in the United States might accept a smaller profit to keep its own customers happy.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:52.000
But what typically happens is that businesses
pass some or all the costs of that tariff on to consumers.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.480
So prices rise.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:56.180
And the consumer ends up paying more for the
same product.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:57.960
Or they just don’t buy the product at all.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.340
But why do governments use tariffs in the
first place?

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:06.820
One reason is to try to protect or prop up
domestic industries that are losing out to imports

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:08.560
from foreign competitors.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:12.720
Another is to retaliate against
unfair trade practices by foreign governments.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.860
For most of the last century, tariffs fell
out of favor.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:19.200
Much of the world came to see freer trade
as a benefit.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.660
It boosted growth.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:22.500
It expanded consumer choices.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.200
It lowered retail prices.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:27.860
The competition fostered and encouraged
innovation.

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:32.120
This made companies more efficient. 
It also made them more profitable.

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:37.840
But, recently tariffs have surged back into
prominence as a favored tool of President Donald Trump.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:41.240
He’s used them more than any president since
Herbert Hoover.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:46.360
He has slapped them not only on China, but
also on U.S. allies like the European Union,

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.240
Mexico, and Canada.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.580
These tariffs mean U.S. producers that import
materials or parts, they have to pay more.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.900
That makes products more expensive.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:58.900
And that leads to fewer sales and more job losses.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:03.260
Tariffs also invite retaliation which has
led to reduced exports.

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:04.400
Take China.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:07.200
The United States is China’s biggest trading
partner.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.600
It imports more than half a trillion dollars
in goods every year.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:14.220
As Americans buy iPhones, clothing, washing
machines, and much more.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:18.100
But the U.S. also exports $120 billion dollars a year to China.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:21.720
These are things like aircraft, machinery, and soybeans
that all head east.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:31.540
So after President Trump imposed tariffs on $250 billion dollars worth of Chinese goods in 2018, China responded with tariffs on U.S. goods.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:34.240
This led to a drop in U.S. exports to China.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:37.640
Predictably, bilateral trade between the two
giants fell.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:42.140
Partly as a result, trade and economic
growth are slowing worldwide.

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:44.860
Traditional trade patterns are shifting too.

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:50.000
U.S. companies are buying more clothes from
Vietnam and more smart phones from South Korea.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.020
China is now getting more of its soybeans
from Brazil and Argentina.

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:57.400
And across the United States, 
farm bankruptcies are on the rise.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:03.100
In response, the U.S. government has pledged nearly $28 billion dollars in subsidies for American farmers.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:07.680
That's about $7 billion more than the government is taking in from China tariffs.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.860
So yes, tariffs offer a potent weapon
for countering unfair trade practices.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:16.440
But tariffs also raise costs for local companies.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:23.280
This leads to lower demand for their products,
it reduces their sales abroad, and costs jobs and profits.

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:25.730
It also unsettles global markets.

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:28.160
So, in the end, who pays for tariffs?

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:31.520
As we've seen, it's usually you, the consumer.

